      "id": "bbc-2026-09-23-chris-mason-from-inside-the-room-how-burnham-survived-first-",
      "title": "Chris Mason: From inside the room, how Burnham survived first Trump meeting",
      "summary": "Burnham seemed nervous at first, knowing how explosive these encounters with Trump can be, writes BBC political editor Chris Mason.",
      "body": "As I crouched between the cameras, determined to catch Donald Trump's eye and ask a question, I listened to a president seeking to be magnanimous.Remember, Trump can and does do warmth when he wants to, as well as acerbic.Prime Minister Andy Burnham, a mayor a matter of months ago, sat next to the most powerful man in the world.Put like that, it sounds believable when he said in advance he was looking forward to the meeting, but there must have been a jab of nerves too.Burnham did look a little nervous as us reporters trooped into the room, but then, for the most part, the compliments flowed from the president.The original plan was for journalists to be invited in at the beginning of the meeting, perhaps just minutes after the men had first met.Instead, we were kept waiting while the private talks took place, which came with the upside that they had more to say about each other and what they had discussed than would otherwise have been the case.Burnham knew, the world knows, how explosive these rolling, free wheeling, live on camera encounters with Trump can go.It is rollercoaster without a harness stuff.Having got myself into the president's eyeline, I waited for the last syllable of Burnham's final word at the end of his opening remarks and I filled the brief silence with a question.Was the prime minister a man you can do business with, I asked the president, given your political differences?\"I do, I think he's a natural business person. We've had a lot of discussions even before this meeting. We've spoken to each other numerous times on the phone. Yeah, I think so, I think so. He gets it. He understands what he has to do.\"The warmth was self evident. Perhaps the Downing Street nerves will have eased a bit.But how long would the questions keep going? This is the president's theatre and he decides how long the public performance lasts. On occasion they are way north of half an hour.This was a meeting on neutral territory, a windowless, nondescript meeting room down a dingy corridor in the basement of the United Nations. This wasn't the Oval Office of the White House. Perhaps that dialled down the sense of occasion a notch.Nonetheless, for how long could the president maintain the bonhomie and sense of agreement? A question about immigration, potentially a point of friction, came and went without incident. So too did the Burnham government's decision, announced by the Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband, who was in the room, to sanction settlements in the West Bank, a decision which infuriated Israel. But on the future of the Chagos islands, the president couldn't hide his view that Britain's decision to cede sovereignty of them to Mauritius and then rent back the military base on Diego Garcia was \"terrible.\"The president added: \"I think the prime minister is going to be looking at [the deal]\" and Burnham replied \"yep\".Ah, interesting.Later, it was my turn to do a short interview with the prime minister on behalf of all of the broadcasters.Burnham emphasised that the meeting was \"very encouraging\" and \"we've established a good connection.\"But what about the Chagos deal? Would it change, I asked. Pointedly, he described it as a deal he had \"inherited\".\"We've got to find a resolution, and so work will continue now…to see where that resolution might lead.\"In other words, it looks like it is going to change.Flashpoints down the track with Trump are near inevitable — barely any leader avoids them with this administration, and Burnham may want to lean into some, in time.And even those he doesn't might lean into him, Chagos possibly one of them.But outwardly Burnham has a relationship with the president he can now build on after this opening face to face conversation.What followed in the prime minister's remaining meetings at the UN were diplomacy by convention, not the wild ride with Trump he had just stepped off and survived.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-23T04:32:59.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/25ce/live/8d688920-b724-11f1-a430-4d16ee157c41.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm158y74n5vxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-23-discord-s-age-verification-era-is-upon-us-despite-community-",
      "title": "Discord’s age-verification era is upon us, despite community backlash",
      "summary": "According to Discord, 90% of users will not have to verify their age.",
      "body": "Starting Wednesday, Discord, the community messaging platform, will roll out age-verification measures to all users.\nThe rollout comes after the company initially delayed its age-verification plans in February due to backlash from users concerned that biometric scans and ID checks could jeopardize their privacy. Those concerns weren’t unfounded; last year, a third-party vendor that works with Discord was breached, potentially exposing the data of 70,000 users’ data, including government ID photos and selfies used for identity verification.\n“Age assurance draws strong opinions and skepticism, especially when it involves sharing biometric information or a form of ID,” Discord CTO Stanislav Vishnevskiy wrote in a blog post, published Tuesday. “At the same time, age assurance laws are expanding, and since my last post we’ve had to launch age assurance in other regions, including in Brazil and in Texas.”\nHowever, Vishnevskiy says more than 90% of Discord users won’t have to verify their age. He explains that Discord will instead use existing account data to detect “signals,” like account tenure (how long an account has existed), device and activity data, and other usage patterns, to determine whether a user belongs in the adult, teen, or unconfirmed age group.\nDiscord says that it doesn’t estimate users’ ages based on message content, profile data, or demographic attributes.\n“The way a typical 15-year-old uses Discord (including how many communities they’re a part of and how they interact with others) looks different in aggregate from how a typical 30-year-old uses Discord,” the company wrote in a separate, technical blog post, also published Tuesday. “Our [machine learning] model has learned these patterns to predict whether a given account likely belongs to an adult or a teen.”\nThis doesn’t mean that Discord is collecting new user data. It’s standard for social media companies to retain this kind of data on their customers, even though it might feel unsettling to see it spelled out so overtly.\nBecause local laws have required Discord to use age-assurance technology in certain regions due to local law, the company was able to train its model on reliably confirmed data linking user behavior to age.\nIf Discord is not confident that a user is an adult, that user can still prove their age without a biometric or ID scan. They can appeal by sharing a credit card or age group data from their Apple App Store or Google Play Store account.\nFor confirmed adults, nothing about the platform will change. Teens accounts, however, will automatically be blocked from accessing age-restricted servers or channels. Message requests from people who aren’t their friends will go to a separate message requests inbox, and teens will get alerts when accepting friend requests from someone they don’t share mutual friends with.\nAs governments around the world continue passing age-verification requirements in an attempt to protect children online, experts warn that these age checks can create a host of new privacy and security issues. That leaves platforms like Discord with a difficult choice: comply or risk sanctions.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAmanda Silberling is a senior writer at TechCrunch covering the intersection of technology and culture. She has also written for publications like Polygon, MTV, the Kenyon Review, NPR, and Business Insider. She is the co-host of Wow If True, a podcast about internet culture, with science fiction author Isabel J. Kim. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she worked as a grassroots organizer, museum educator, and film festival coordinator. She holds a B.A. in English from the University of Pennsylvania and served as a Princeton in Asia Fellow in Laos.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Amanda by emailing amanda@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at @amanda.100 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Amanda Silberling",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-23T03:31:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/09/discord-app-logo.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/22/discords-age-verification-era-is-upon-us-despite-community-backlash/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-23-trump-wants-ai-to-be-rebranded-as-super-intelligence-will-it",
      "title": "Trump wants AI to be rebranded as super intelligence. Will it work?",
      "summary": "Some people close to the president have started to use the term but experts say it is unlikely to catch on.",
      "body": "US President Donald Trump says the term artificial intelligence (AI) makes the technology \"sound fake\" and wants it to be called \"super intelligence\".\"Welcome to the new world of super intelligence - SI,\" Trump said during a wide-ranging speech to the United Nations General Assembly in New York on Tuesday.While some people close to the president immediately started to use the term, experts have told the BBC it is unlikely to gain widespread traction in the industry because it is a name typically used to refer to more advanced systems.The announcement is Trump's latest rebranding move after he had the names of Lake Ontario and the Gulf of Mexico changed on US maps and federal communications.\"From this point forward, all of United States' documents, and hopefully the world's, will be changed to use the more accurate term 'super' as opposed to 'artificial'. So it's 'super intelligence',\" Trump said.In the past week, Trump asked his social media followers to vote on other potential new names for AI including superior intelligence, extreme intelligence and supreme intelligence.After a series of polls on his Truth Social platform, super intelligence came out as the winner.The purpose of renaming AI - and whether it will be adopted by the technology industry - is unclear.AI refers to computer technology that allows machines to do tasks that usually require human thinking.Meanwhile, super intelligence has a specific meaning within the tech community.The term is often linked to the author and philosopher Nick Bostrom, who more than a decade ago defined the idea as an \"intellect\" that is superior to humans in all forms. It is still entirely hypothetical.The rebranding is \"misleading\", as super intelligence typically refers to a system that has the ability to improve by itself, said digital ethics lecturer Simon Coghlan from The University of Melbourne.\"I doubt 'SI' will stick, in part because it exaggerates the current capacities of AI,\" he said.\"A superintelligent system, if it came about, would be regarded by experts as the most consequential technology ever made,\" Coghlan said.\"Not all AI platforms today exude super intelligence\", said computer scientist Kokil Jaidka, adding that the new name \"borrows the prestige of a term researchers reserve for a much bigger leap\".National University of Singapore computer science lecturer Ben Leong said: \"My guess is that professionals will want to save 'SI' for a higher level of AI, not so much to describe AI today. It doesn't feel like it's at that level yet.\"But some in Trump's circle have already started to use the new term.\"The Super Intelligence (SI) President has spoken! The United States is and will remain the global leader in SI,\" US Chief Technology Officer Ethan Klein wrote on social media.While Trump's ambassador to the UN Michael Waltz later said: \"If you didn't see the president's speech, it's now super intelligence. I think he's absolutely right.\"However, when asked for his thoughts on the new name, Canada's minister of AI Evan Solomon said: \"Canadians are not enthusiastic, at all, about renaming things,\" in an apparent reference to Trump renaming Lake Ontario.",
