      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-22-obscura-vpn-that-can-t-log-your-activity",
      "title": "Obscura: VPN that can't log your activity",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://obscura.com/#faq-technical\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49807003\nPoints: 139\n# Comments: 111",
      "body": "New  Now available on Linux Now on Linux\nMeet Obscura: the first VPN that  log your activity\nGet Started      Use with Wireguard\nVPNs know more about you than they should. So we made one that doesn’t.\nEven “no-log” VPNs can track you, since they see both who you are and\nwhat you do. Obscura is built such that we can’t see your\ntraffic in the first place.\nTraditional VPNs see your identity and your browsing historyTraditional VPNJacob Williams96.96.216.135Visited tumblr.com just now7961 Kulas Estate, Danville USAJacob Williams96.96.216.135Visited pinterest.com just now7961 Kulas Estate, Danville USAJacob Williams96.96.216.135Visited apple.com just now7961 Kulas Estate, Danville USAJacob Williams96.96.216.135Visited microsoft.com just now7961 Kulas Estate, Danville USAJacob Williams96.96.216.135Visited ebay.com just now7961 Kulas Estate, Danville USAObscura only sees your IP address and never your browsing historyObscuraAccount OBS-2084891996.96.216.135Exit NodeVisited tumblr.com just nowVisited pinterest.com just nowVisited apple.com just nowVisited microsoft.com just nowVisited ebay.com just now             It’s simple:\nWe can’t leak what we don’t have.\nObscura VPN  doesn’t log your IP address\ncan’t see your internet traffic, ever. It’s impossible\nLog in with just a randomized account number. No names,\nno emails, no phone numbers necessary.\nCredit card info? Not required.\nEasily pay using Bitcoin’s Lightning Network for better privacy,\ninstant payments, and lower transaction fees. We also accept Monero.\nBy blending in with regular internet traffic, Obscura avoids being\ndetected by network filters – keeping your internet access unrestricted.\nPrivate by Design:  Our Two-Party VPN Protocol\nBy using a fully-independent exit hop, Obscura keeps who you are and\nwhat you do separate. More technical details here.\nObscura never sees your trafficObscura’s servers relay your connection to exit servers but can never decrypt your traffic.Your traffic is always end-to-end encrypted via WireGuard® to the exit server.Exit hops never see who you areExit servers (run by Mullvad) connect you to the internet but never see your personal info.Obscura masks your real IP address when relaying to the exit server.       FAQs  Questions?We’re here to help.\nHere are the most common questions we’re asked, but if we haven’t\ncovered yours, don’t hesitate to reach out via one of our handles.\nWhat makes Obscura different from existing VPNs?            What user data can Obscura see?            How do I know Obscura does what it claims?            How much does Obscura cost, what payment methods are accepted?           How many devices can I use Obscura on?            How does Obscura differ from my VPN’s multihop option?            How does Obscura compare to Tor?            What server locations are available?            What relay servers does Obscura offer?            How does the macOS app work? Does it have kernel-level access?            Give me the technical details!            Can I generate a WireGuard configuration to use Obscura?            How do I download Obscura?                 I’m Carl, head-janitor of Obscura 👋\nThanks for reaching the bottom — not everyone makes it.\nI’m incredibly lucky to have the help of a crack(ed) team of privacy optimists to build Obscura.\nAmong us, we’ve served on the Nix RFC Steering Committee, implemented the 64bit random number generator for the Go standard library, fixed critical vulnerabilities for hardware security tokens, won bounties for Monero bugs, and contributed to Bitcoin for reproducible builds.\nBut while privacy and digital sovereignty in some worlds has made leaps and bounds, VPNs have been left behind; peddling privacy based on promises instead of privacy baked into the architecture.\nSo we’re taking our skills to build Obscura: a VPN you can depend on to get the most out of our glorious digital commons – the internet. It’s the VPN we’ve always wanted to use, and it’s the kind of privacy we believe everyone should have access to.\nThanks for reading – ping me at carl@obscura.net for any questions, and I’ll see you on the free and open internet. 🏄\nI fight for the users.\nOur team learning laser-cutting at the NYC Resistor hackerspace\nStart using Obscura for macOS, iOS, Android, Windows, and Linux today, or\njoin our waitlist for other platforms.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Flimm",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-22T19:40:28.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://obscura.com/og.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://obscura.com/#faq-technical",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
