      "id": "bbc-2026-09-21-my-army-dream-was-ended-by-childhood-eye-surgery",
      "title": "'My army dream was ended by childhood eye surgery'",
      "summary": "Some 114,000 Army application were rejected on medical grounds in the past five years, Freedom of Information figures show.",
      "body": "Jackson spent his teenage years in the Army Cadets where he was recognised in his unit as the best shot.He applied to join the Army Reserve, motivated by a desire to \"give back\" to a country which he said had given him \"so much\", but he was rejected.\"It's very frustrating, especially when you know that you are fit and healthy and you haven't even been given the opportunity to fail,\" he said.There were 108,020 applications to the British Army Regular Forces in the year leading up to September 2025.Separate figures obtained under a Freedom of Information (FOI) request to the Ministry of Defence (MoD) showed more than 114,000 applications to the Army were rejected on medical grounds in the past five years.Psychiatry, eye and musculoskeletal conditions were some of the most common reasons for rejection.An MOD spokesperson said: \"The majority of these figures cover years before the substantial changes the government has made to remove over 100 outdated and unnecessary recruitment policies.\"Inspired by other family members who had served in the armed forces, he applied to join the RAF, hoping to train as either aircrew or a mechanic.However, his application was rejected because he uses an inhaler.Smith's appeal included a letter from his GP which said he had an \"unlimited exercise tolerance\".The letter said: \"He is a fit and active young man with a strong ambition to join.\"His appeal was unsuccessful.The review said: \"All candidates, irrespective of role, need to be a minimum of 12 months treatment, symptom and medication-free.\"For Smith, the decision brought his life-long ambition to an end.\"The dream has ended,\" he said.\"I've been told no. I don't know what to do with myself.\"Prof Neil Greenberg, president of the Society of Occupational Medicine, said: \"The military has to have strict standards of physical and psychological health.\"It requires people to go all around the globe, often at short notice, and to put up with environments that really are quite unpleasant.\"It is vital that soldiers can perform to a very high level because if it goes wrong, it could mean loss of life.\"Former British Army Lt Col Stuart Crawford, who was previously a defence spokesman for the Scottish National Party (SNP), said the military was \"losing out on talent\".He said: \"Recruitment policies must be in place, of course, but we need to be careful that we are not turning away talent because of minor medical issues.\"He said this was \"increasingly dangerous\" in a time of global instability.\"We're always being told that we could be at war and we are nowhere near rising to that challenge in terms of our recruitment,\" Crawford said.An MoD spokesperson said: \"Each application is considered on a case-by-case basis by specialist clinicians, assessing the severity of any condition and the individual's ability to train, deploy and operate.\"Jackson said the MoD needed to go \"much further\" in its review of recruitment policies.\"We're blanketing out thousands of young men and women,\" he said.Jackson is currently studying agricultural management at the Royal Agricultural University in Cirencester.\"I'm in a lucky situation being at university where I have the time to fight my case alongside my studies,\" he said.\"But for many people, it's just not feasible to spend hours a week trying to fight the system.\"",
