      "id": "theverge-2026-09-21-amazon-doesn-t-trust-meta-s-muse-ai-agent",
      "title": "Amazon doesn’t trust Meta’s Muse AI agent",
      "summary": "Meta's Muse AI agent has been blocked from shopping Amazon on behalf of its users, GeekWire reports. A popup message started appearing on Sunday for Muse users saying that \"continued access by an unauthorized AI agent violates Amazon's Conditions of Use, to which our customers have agreed.\"\nAccording to GeekWire, Meta didn't notify Amazon that Muse would access its store. Amazon also expressed pri",
      "body": "Meta’s Muse AI agent has been blocked from shopping Amazon on behalf of its users, GeekWire reports. A popup message started appearing on Sunday for Muse users saying that “continued access by an unauthorized AI agent violates Amazon’s Conditions of Use, to which our customers have agreed.”\nAccording to GeekWire, Meta didn’t notify Amazon that Muse would access its store. Amazon also expressed privacy and security concerns over Muse failing to identify itself when it browses, and seemingly capturing customer credentials.\n“We think it’s fairly straightforward that third-party applications that offer to make purchases on behalf of customers from other businesses should operate openly and respect service provider decisions about whether or not to participate,” an unnamed Amazon spokesperson said in a statement to GeekWire. We have reached out to Meta for comment.\nWhen Meta launched Muse earlier this month, it said the AI agent cannot see secure login details or card payment information. We used it during testing to successfully purchase tank tops from Amazon while other products were in the basket. Some privacy concerns have emerged since launch, however, with reports that Muse can see the contents of user messages despite not having the necessary access permission enabled.\nThis marks Amazon’s latest effort to fend off competition from rival agentic AI services and keep its customers shopping with the retailer directly. It sued Perplexity in November last year in a similar attempt to keep the Amazon platform out of the AI search provider’s Comet shopping experience, with a judge siding with Perplexity on the case in August. Confirmation emails from Amazon have also started looking notably sparse since July, with specific item names and product images being omitted to limit that information from being mined by external AI services.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Amazon",
        "Meta",
        "News",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Jess Weatherbed",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-21T09:21:47.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/Amazon_Prime_Illo.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/998078/amazon-blocks-meta-muse-ai-agent-shopping",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-21-world-gurning-championship-winners-crowned",
      "title": "World Gurning Championship winners crowned",
      "summary": "The World Gurning Championship is held annually in the small Cumbrian town of Egremont.",
      "body": "Image source, Steven R WoodImage caption, Will Mellor came second in the men's competitionByJason Arunn MurugesuNorth East and CumbriaActor Will Mellor has come a surprise second in the World Gurning Championships, pipped to the main prize by local man Ged Eccles who had come close but never before won.Part of the annual Egremont Crab Fair in Cumbria, which began in 1267, the gurning competition has been a fixture since the 1500s. Competitors are judged on their ability to pull the ugliest or most grotesque face.The Hollyoaks and Mr Bates vs The Post Office star won second place in the men's competition after attending as part of a programme he was filming. Eccles said he felt \"ecstatic\" to have finally won the men's competition. It was his first victory since becoming the junior champion in 1985.He came second in 2023 and was third in 2016 and 2009 and said he believed he had won this year because of the extra practice he had put in.  Image source, Steven R WoodImage caption, Ged Eccles won this year's world championships\"I practise in front of a mirror for maybe three to four hours a day,\" he said. \"I've been doing that since the end of May.\"He said he did this to loosen up his neck, cheekbone and jaw bone muscles in order to get his lip on to his nose. \"And that's what I did,\" he said. Eccles said he hoped to defend his title next year and that he planned to continue gurning \"until the day I can't do it anymore\". One of the event's organisers, Dan Nixon, said it was good to see Eccles finally win. He said there were about 50 entrants to the men's, women's and junior versions of the competition and that up to 800 people had attended.\"I think everyone in Egremont takes part in the Crab Fair in some way,\" he said.Image source, Steven R WoodImage caption, Claire Lister is a seasoned winner, Dan Nixon saidLocal woman Claire Lister won the women's competition again after losing her crown last year.The nine-time champion said: \"I'm absolutely delighted to have won again, I'm really proud and it still feels just as special every time.\"It's just a bit of fun and keeps the Egremont Crab Fair tradition going.\"Lister said she does not train or practise for the competition but just turns up on the day and gives it her best.\"I think after doing it for so many years it just comes naturally to me now,\" she said.Nixon described her as \"a seasoned winner\".The competition's organising committee have already started planning next year's event which will be its 760th.\"We're very much hoping that it's going to be a really, really big spectacular,\" he said.Follow BBC Cumbria on X, external, Facebook, external, Nextdoor and Instagram, external and listen to Radio Cumbria on BBC Sounds. Get in touchDo you have a story suggestion for BBC North East & Cumbria?Related storiesRelated internet links",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-21T09:10:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5294/live/6728a650-b5a3-11f1-bc1f-3f186ca4140c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crjw5p03qqpvo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
