    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-20-kde-turns-30-and-someone-s-brought-an-ai-native-desktop-prop",
      "title": "KDE turns 30 and someone's brought an AI-native desktop proposal",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/09/18/kde-turns-30-and-someones-brought-an-ai-native-desktop-proposal/5297282\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49773439\nPoints: 31\n# Comments: 37",
      "body": "Akademy talk imagines Plasma assembling itself around a personal model of each user\nKDE's annual conference takes place this weekend at Graz University of Technology in Austria, with an AI-native desktop proposal likely to divide attendees.The KDE project is celebrating its 30th anniversary this year, giving delegates at the Akademy conference in Graz another reason to raise a glass. The conference begins on Saturday, September 19, and registration remains open.Although Xfce 1.0 appeared slightly earlier, its initial releases were proprietary, making what was originally known as the Kool Desktop Environment one of the earliest entirely FOSS desktop environments for Linux. Version 1.0 was released in July 1998. Although created for Linux, KDE software now runs on several other operating systems.\nThe potentially contentious proposal comes in a Sunday afternoon talk entitled What would it take? A lovable, sovereign, AI-native KDE, presented by longtime KDE contributors Eva Brucherseifer and Jan Muehlig.\nIn 2003, they conducted a usability study of the then newly released KDE 3.1. Of the 60 office workers tested, none of whom had prior Linux experience, 87 percent said they enjoyed working with KDE.The talk has three parts: what KDE learned – or failed to learn – from that study; the case for an \"AI-native\" desktop; and the importance of a sovereign European computing stack. It is the middle section that may ruffle feathers. The outline says: \"A desktop that loves you back has to know you. Personal AI has crossed the threshold where the desktop itself can be compiled per user, per device, per moment, from a small portable model of the user – what we are sketching as Kadai: an encrypted, vendor-agnostic personal kernel from which Plasma assembles each Activity.\"Plasma is uniquely well-fit for this – Activities, Plasmoids, the existing scripting surface – but a handful of upstream changes (declarative reconciliation, per-widget capabilities, richer Activity metadata) would turn KDE into the first shell that treats AI as infrastructure rather than as a chat box bolted to the side.\"This is very much not what this vulture wants from a desktop computer. It may win admirers, but seems more likely to polarize the audience.Writer and AI critic David Gerard points out the proposal's resemblance to the Resonant Computing Manifesto, launched in December 2025 at Wired's Big Interview in San Francisco. Gerard criticized the manifesto later that month.Gerard also notes that contributors to the Trinity Desktop Environment, a fork of KDE 3, have begun dabbling with AI-generated code – potentially disappointing those who viewed Trinity as an escape route. Anyone determined to keep AI off their desktop might instead check on the progress of the Sonic Desktop Environment, which we covered in July. ®",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "pndy",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-20T07:38:37.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://image.theregister.com/5297398.jpg?imageId=5297398&x=0&y=0&cropw=100&croph=100&panox=0&panoy=0&panow=100&panoh=100&width=1200&height=683",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theregister.com/software/2026/09/18/kde-turns-30-and-someones-brought-an-ai-native-desktop-proposal/5297282",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-20-premier-league-fans-and-players-honour-noah-woods",
      "title": "Premier League fans and players honour Noah Woods",
      "summary": "Everton and Ipswich supporters and players pay tribute to the three-year-old, who died this week.",
      "body": "Premier League fans and players honour Noah WoodsImage source, Action Images/ReutersImage caption, Ipswich and Everton fans stood to applaud in Noah's memory as a picture of him was shown on the big screenA tribute to three-year-old Noah Woods was shown on the big screen ahead of the Everton vs Ipswich Town Premier League match, while players wore black armbands in his memory.The toddler became separated from a relative after visiting a playground in Merriam Close, Brantham, on Tuesday afternoon, Suffolk Police said.About 1,300 members of the public joined a search to find Noah, who police said was non-verbal, partially deaf and had been undergoing an assessment for autism.Met Police divers recovered his body from a lake, known locally as Decoy Pond, on Wednesday afternoon and Suffolk Police said his death was not being treated as suspicious.Three minutes into the game, won 1-0 by the home side, both sets of supporters stood to applaud in memory of Noah, Everton said in a post on X, external.Captain Dara O'Shea and goalkeeper Christian Walton were among the club delegation who laid flowers at the playground where he went missing.Image source, Action Images/ReutersImage caption, Ipswich Town defender Leif Davis donated £2,000 to a fundraising appeal set up in aid of Noah Woods's familyTown defender Leif Davis donated £2,000 to the fundraising appeal set up to support Noah's family. He spoke to the BBC after the Everton game, describing how he joined search efforts after Ipswich played Arsenal on Tuesday and stayed until 03:00 BST the following morning.Davis explained his recent experience of becoming a father himself made the situation feel close to home.He said: \"Even if I wasn't a new father, I would have been out there... It's something that I would want someone else someone to do if it was my little girl or boy.\"It was a sad thing, it really hit us, and I hope his family and everyone close to him are strong and stay safe as well.\"On Thursday, Noah's father Rhys Woods paid tribute to his son in a message shared on a community Facebook group.He wrote: \"There are no words to describe the pain of this loss, we are all absolutely heartbroken, Noah was the sweetest and most innocent little boy, with nothing but love to give, and we are going to miss him so very much.\"Image source, Family handoutImage caption, Noah Woods was at a playground with a relative before he went missingSuffolk Police said on Thursday that the body of the child recovered by police divers had been formally identified as Noah, subject to confirmation at formal inquest proceedings.The force said police still have a number of inquiries to complete before submitting a file for the coroner.Meanwhile, a fundraising page set up by Good Dads Club, a community of fathers based in nearby Colchester, had received more than £100,000 in donations from a total of more than 6,000 supporters by Saturday evening.Do you have a story suggestion for Suffolk? Contact us below.Get in touchMore on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-20T07:35:08.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/ec69/live/98686420-b469-11f1-bc1f-3f186ca4140c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/crz6z7e0zzqjo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-20-fleming-on-england-discipline-stokes-late-arrival-and-coachi",
      "title": "Fleming on England discipline, Stokes, late arrival and coaching styles",
      "summary": "Read what England's new Test coach Stephen Fleming had to say about Bazball, Ben Stokes, the team's discipline issues and his own philosophy.",
      "body": "Fleming on England discipline, Stokes, late arrival and coaching stylesImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Fleming has joined England on a four-year dealByMatthew HenryBBC Sport journalistStephen Fleming has given his first interviews since becoming England's Test coach. The New Zealander, who replaces his close friend Brendon McCullum, spoke at length - covering Bazball, Ben Stokes' departure, England's discipline problems, his own coaching style and more. Here's what he had to say...Fleming on Carse and England's discipline problemsEngland have been dogged by off-field issues in recent times. Harry Brook was involved in an altercation with a nightclub bouncer last year, Ben Duckett was videoed apparently drunk in Australia, Stokes and Gus Atkinson broke a team curfew and Brydon Carse was investigated by police after an incident in Derby.Fleming said: \"It's been disappointing, because I understand what people are working towards. These events can derail a lot of things and divert attention from what really matters. \"When I look at epitomising an international cricketer and what they should stand for, it's Joe [Root]. \"When he sits in an interview and openly expresses his disappointment, or uses \"hacked off\", I know there's work to be done. \"Also, the belonging, identity, what are we doing here together and how we can be the best version of ourselves. A lot of that work has begun. \"That's one of the key things for me to develop and my work with Joe has begun.\"On Carse, specifically, Fleming said the bowler is available for selection but added: \"There are standards. We pick on runs and wickets primarily, but I think it's foolish not to also say there are standards that need to be set.\"On his relationship with McCullumImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Fleming joked he is like McCullum's big brother Fleming has a long-standing relationship with McCullum as a former team-mate, friend and business partner. He replaces his fellow Black Cap, whose tenure as Test coach began with stunning highs before turning sour with the Ashes defeat and his sacking this summer.\"He was heart-sore it had worked out that way, but he was incredibly positive about the experience and opportunity. \"He's been unwavering on that. His belief about where these teams can go is strong, and that was a major part when I was talking to him about doing the role. \"I love his confidence and aggressive nature. I'm more measured. \"Somewhere in between we've got a beautiful working relationship with a huge amount of respect. \"I have so much respect for what Ben Stokes and Brendon did for this English side. They changed the landscape of cricket and that's a really powerful thing.\"On what went wrong with BazballMcCullum's side beat New Zealand and South Africa in their first summer before a memorable win in Pakistan. They were unable to win any of their five-Test series against India and Australia, however.\"I was in awe of what that team were doing and how they were doing it. \"The last 12 months, the evolution – teams caught up, there was talent lost, a lot of reasons. That's now my opportunity. \"There was a thousand wickets lost – [James] Anderson, [Stuart] Broad, then [Chris] Woakes. \"When you do something special and you start doing it differently, everybody else takes a bit of time, then they catch up. \"The pressure then goes on to evolution and that can create some challenges. That's my take on it. \"The Ashes wasn't what they wanted and that is what this team is measured on. \"All I can think about is now my opportunity to pick it up, and maybe add my style, which is looking at personality, a little bit more measured.\"Image source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Stokes and McCullum were united as captain and coach in 2022. Their tenure ended after this summer's defeat by New ZealandStokes, who announced his shock retirement in June, played under Fleming at Chennai Super Kings in the Indian Premier League. Fleming revealed the pair spoke at Durham earlier this week.Fleming said: \"He'd just come off another monster bowling spell. \"He was in a great space, really happy, enjoying his time with Durham. \"He's got a good winter planned with the family and is very much at peace with his decision. When a player makes that decision to retire, I respect that decision 100%.\"To try to turn that around and question that is wrong. I know how much goes into that type of decision, and I respect that.\"Fleming on his wait to arriveFleming was named England coach on 30 July but did not take charge of the recent series win over Pakistan, instead following from home.Fleming said: \"There were commitments and family commitments I had that I just wasn't in a position to break. That's more about me as a person, than dropping everything and coming over here. \"I watched and connected with Joe and the team straight away once it was approved – I've got a stress fracture in my left ear from phone calls. \"Watching remotely, I won't say it was an advantage, but watching them operate with eyes wide open as to what was expected, the opportunity, the challenges, and there was also another part – introducing Joe back into captaincy, and Marcus Trescothick the opportunity to do the head coach's role. \"That's part of why I love the game, bringing people forward, developing people, leadership, and they did brilliantly.\"It gave me an opportunity to catch my breath, and then start pretty fresh with a clear mind about what's ahead.\"Fleming on his own coaching styleImage source, Getty ImagesImage caption, Fleming led Chennai Super Kings to five Indian Premier League titles before leaving his role as head coach after this year's tournament Though Fleming has extensive experience in franchise cricket, he has never coached a red-ball side. He was New Zealand captain for 80 of his 111 Tests, however. On the playing style he wants, Fleming said: \"If people were watching a team, I'd love people to say they are well-planned, there's a discipline and a really strong basic structure to what we're doing, but there's also a bit of flair. \"That's not necessarily me and the way I played, but it's what I loved about this English side when they started playing like this. \"It's taking the best of that and adding that planning aspect, so if supporters could look at us and say 'yeah, they are disciplined, well-planned and competitive'. That's a good start.\"The greatest thing for a team to do is to be able to play conditions and situations. \"One thing this team has earned is the ability to be positive. One of the other aspects is winning the tough moments and making sure we don't just play entertaining cricket and win moments, but win games.\"On the Ashes and the World Test Championship Fleming's first series in charge is against South Africa in December. After two Tests in Bangladesh and a one-off anniversary Test in Melbourne in the winter, attention will then turn to next summer's Ashes. Fleming rejected the idea English cricket is too obsessed by beating Australia.\"No, because I have an obsession with it as well,\" he said. \"New Zealand has an obsession with it, we love it.\"We want to beat Aussie. We're heavily linked to England performances. We have a deep desire to beat Australia in everything, as the little cousin. \"We are linked, and I'm incredibly excited about being part of it. You don't think you're ever going to get that opportunity. \"To have that opportunity and be part of it, I can't wait. I want to be my best self and have a team that represents itself in the best possible way. I've got a vision of that and it's aspirational. It will be brilliant.\"On the World Test Championship, an event England are yet to reach the final of, he said: \"England could do much better than that.\"And even attitude just around the over rate, I think, comes down to identity and style of play. \"And if that's one of the pinnacle trophies in world cricket, why would we not be trying to get it along with every other marquee event that comes our way?\"More on this story",
