      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-19-chess-atlas",
      "title": "Chess Atlas",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://chess-timeline.vercel.app/gallery.html\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49764631\nPoints: 22\n# Comments: 8",
      "body": "c. 700 AD – 1900\nAfrasiab · Charlemagne · Lewis · Vizagapatam · Northern Upright\nThe oldest surviving chess pieces. From abstract 7th-century Samarkand to the expressive Lewis Chessmen of Norse origin, through ornate Indian export sets and Chinese puzzle balls. Ivory was the material of courts and conquerors.\nPuzzle Ball — George II\nSelenus · Staunton · Man Ray · Hartwig · Ernst · Calvert · St. George\nThe material of standardisation. From Selenus’s 1616 treatise through the Régence, St. George, and Barleycorn patterns to Nathaniel Cook’s 1849 Staunton, which became the universal standard. Then the modernists: Man Ray, Hartwig at the Bauhaus, Max Ernst.\nSasanian · Persian · Meissen · Kändler · Esser · Wedgwood\nFrom ancient Persian and Sasanian abstract forms, among the earliest known chess pieces, to Meissen under Kändler and Esser, and Wedgwood’s neoclassical jasperware. Clay was chess’s first medium; porcelain became its most precious.\nFischer vs Spassky — G5\nFischer vs Spassky — G13\nMan Ray · Calder · Fischer · Gehry · Hadid · Skyline Chess\nThe century of artists and architects. Man Ray’s geometric brass, Calder’s wire sculptures, Fischer’s legendary 1972 match against Spassky. Then Gehry for Tiffany, Zaha Hadid’s parametric towers, Skyline Chess’s city skylines cast in metal.\nNoguchi · Swarovski · Murano · Nendo / Baccarat\nTransparency as concept. Noguchi’s Lucite pieces for “The Imagery of Chess” exhibition, Swarovski’s precision-cut crystal, Murano’s hand-blown tradition, and Nendo’s radical reimagining of Baccarat’s Harcourt glass.\nYoko Ono — Play It By Trust\nDuchamp · Dalí · Ono · Hirst · Whiteread · Kruger · Emin · Kusama\nChess as art object. Duchamp quit art for chess. Dalí cast surreal fingers. Yoko Ono made both sides white. The RS&A “Art of Chess” commissions brought in Hirst, Whiteread, Kruger, and Emin. Plus Graves, Rashid, Hermès, Kusama, LEGO.",
