      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-18-y-combinator-insurance-tech-alum-angle-health-hits-2-7b-valu",
      "title": "Y Combinator insurance tech alum Angle Health hits $2.7B valuation",
      "summary": "Angle Health has grown to 5,000 customers and become profitable by helping small businesses get \"level-funded\" health insurance.",
      "body": "1:24 PM PDT · September 18, 2026\nImage Credits:TravelCouples / Getty Images\nThese days, it’s rare to hear of a startup founded in 2019, or one not focused on AI agents, raising a hefty Series C. But health insurance startup Angle Health announced Friday that it raised a $200 million Series C, alongside a $400 million tender offer, at a $2.7 billion valuation. The round was led by Vitruvian Partners with participation from Town Hall Ventures, Blumberg Capital, Portage Ventures, PruVen Capital, and Y Combinator. The tender offer allows employees to cash out some of their shares, the company told TechCrunch. It expects the round to close later this month.\nAngle Health, a winter 2020 YC alum, helps small businesses obtain and manage what’s known as “level-funded health plans.” These are plans that land between fully insured and self-funded plans. In fully insured plans, the carrier takes all the risks; such plans are more expensive but costs are predictable. With self-funded plans, the company covers the expenses and costs can be unpredictable. With level-funded plans, businesses make predictable payments to carriers, are insured against higher-than-expected costs, and can potentially save money by getting a share of the surplus back if expenses stay low.\nAngle says these plans can make health insurance more affordable for small businesses. It’s an AI-powered platform that helps small businesses choose and manage plans, integrating with payroll and HR systems. The startup says it serves over 5,000 businesses and is profitable.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Julie Bort",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T20:24:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/GettyImages-1214954967.jpg?resize=1200,612",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/y-combinator-insurance-tech-alum-angle-health-hits-2-7b-valuation/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-18-world-model-companies-are-keeping-a-lot-of-secrets",
      "title": "World model companies are keeping a lot of secrets",
      "summary": "Everyone in the world-models space is sitting on a pile of cash and a ton of buzz, but good luck getting anyone — from the founders to their own data suppliers — to tell you what they're actually building.",
      "body": "This week, I moderated a panel on world models at the All In conference (no relation to the podcast), and it gave me a chance to dig into one of the most mysterious corners of the AI world. The big players in the space are Yann LeCun’s AMI Labs and Fei-Fei Li’s World Labs — and while both have accumulated a lot of buzz and funding, they also rank pretty low on the trying-to-make-money scale.\nAt their core, world models are about automating spatial intelligence, so the field could head in lots of exciting and lucrative directions, from robotics to interactive video to more complex self-driving systems.\nBut when I started to press on where we would actually see the tech commercialized, things got foggy. The closest thing I found to an authority was Michael Rabbat, a co-founder of AMI Labs and the company’s VP of World Models, who joined me on the panel. But when I pressed him on exactly what the company was working on, he was cagey. “We’ll talk about it when we’re ready to talk about it.” Over email, he clarified, “We’re still in a research and building phase, so we’re not talking publicly about any product plans or timeline.”\nTo be fair, AMI is less than a year old, so it’s fair enough to keep quiet. But this sort of caginess extends to the whole world-modeling space. World Labs’ Marble is probably the most fully developed product in the space, and its demos range from straightforward media creation, building explorable environments for video games, or CGI effects. There are robotics use cases too, but the whole platform seems more designed to demonstrate capabilities.\nThat secrecy even extends to these companies’ suppliers. On the sidelines of the same conference, I spoke to Alex de Vigan, CEO of Physicl — a data supplier for the burgeoning world model business. He says he knows Physicl’s data has been useful for whatever they’re building, but he’s still in the dark about what exactly that is. “I wish they would tell us more. We could build more useful data if we knew what they were working on,” de Vigan told me.\nPart of the mystery comes from how versatile world models are as an idea. The simplest version is a navigable map of the world, similar to the AI models that power self-driving cars. But the same modeling approach that helps a Waymo weave through traffic could also help a humanoid robot carry boxes, or turn a few minutes of video footage into an explorable environment. AMI has already dipped its toe in manufacturing, biomedicine, robotics, and even AI software for doctors through its Nabia partnership. Surely it won’t pursue all of those — but maybe one or two of them are standing out?\nNo one doubts that there are lots of viable businesses to be built on world model tech — and as long as it’s easy to fundraise, there’s no particular pressure to focus on one. In fact, there’s good reason not to. If AMI announced tomorrow that they had built a humanoid OpenClaw or a next-generation Hollywood rendering system, a lot of other labs would suddenly be very interested in the space. Soon, the lab would face potential competition from the other world model companies, the neolabs, and even OpenAI and Anthropic.\nIn some ways, it’s the flip side of all that easy fundraising. Your competitors can fundraise, too — and the same money that lets you build under the radar is also funding lots of potential rivals once the path to market becomes clear. But even if that competition is inevitable, it’s best if you delay it for as long as possible, which means keeping quiet about exactly what you’re building.\nCixin Liu fans will recognize this as a dark forest scenario: If you don’t know who else is in the woods, it’s best not to attract attention.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nRussell Brandom has been covering the tech industry since 2012, with a focus on platform policy and emerging technologies. He previously worked at The Verge and Rest of World, and has written for Wired, The Awl and MIT’s Technology Review.\nHe can be reached at russell.brandom@techcrunch.com or on Signal at 412-401-5489.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Russell Brandom",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T20:18:14.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/ALLIN_16-09-AM_melanieolmsteadphotographe-635.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/18/world-model-companies-are-keeping-a-lot-of-secrets/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-at-least-21-killed-after-vehicle-loaded-with-explosives-rams",
      "title": "At least 21 killed after vehicle loaded with explosives rams Pakistan mosque",
      "summary": "A four-year-old child is among those killed in the attack, during which gunmen also opened fire on a police facility.",
      "body": "A car-bomb attack on a mosque at a police compound in north-western Pakistan has killed at least 21 people and injured more than 100, officials say.Fifteen police officers and a four-year-old child are among those killed in the suicide attack, which also involved gunmen firing on the police facility in the city of Kohat in the province of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan's APP news agency reported.Video found by BBC Verify shows the site covered in rubble and people tending to casualties. Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Police suggested a faction of the Tehrik-e-Taliban Pakistan (TTP), also known as the Pakistani Taliban, carried out the attack. A car loaded with explosives rammed into the entrance of the mosque at about 13:15 local time (08:15 GMT), police say. Friday prayers were taking place at the time.At least six of the attackers were killed in the explosion and the gun battle that followed, according to a Khyber Pakhtunkhwa Police spokesperson.In a post on X, the force said it was \"determined to foil the nefarious designs of Fitna-al-Khawarij\" - the designation used by officials to refer to the Pakistani Taliban.The group was set up in 2007 with the aim of enforcing its strict version of Islamic law and is allied with al-Qaeda and the Afghan Taliban. Pakistan's President Asif Ali Zardari expressed \"his best wishes for the swift recovery of those injured in the explosion\" in a statement, and called for the \"complete elimination\" of foreign-backed terrorists.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-18T20:17:50.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/629b/live/fef4ee20-b356-11f1-bc1f-3f186ca4140c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckn9wenz8wjwo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-mcilroy-in-mix-heading-into-weekend-at-wentworth",
      "title": "McIlroy in mix heading into weekend at Wentworth",
      "summary": "Rory McIlroy stays in contention with a five-under 67 to sit four strokes behind second-round leader Ryan Gerard at the BMW PGA Championship at Wentworth.",
      "body": "BMW PGA Championship - round two-12 R Gerard (US); -10 A Scott (Aus), F Celli (Ita); -9 M Elvira (Spa), A Rai (Eng)Selected others: -8 R Fox (NZ), R Fisher (Eng), R McIlroy (NI), I Elvira (Spa), JJ Spaun (US); -7  M Wallace (Eng); -6 S Straka (Aut), A Noren (Swe); -5 L Aberg (Swe), V Hovland (Nor); -4 D Law (Sco), R MacIntyre (Sco), S Lowry (Ire), M Fitzpatrick (Eng); -3 J Rose (Eng)Full leaderboardRory McIlroy stayed in contention with a five-under 67 to sit four strokes behind second-round leader Ryan Gerard at the BMW PGA Championship at Wentworth.The Northern Irishman made three birdies in his opening five holes and recovered from dropped shots on the eighth and 16th by picking up four strokes on the back nine, including birdies on the 17th and 18th holes.Reigning Masters champion McIlroy's hopes of winning a record-equalling eighth Race to Dubai title were also buoyed by the withdrawal of Patrick Reed after nine holes. The American, who currently leads the standings on the DP World Tour, had been well placed after the first round but was forced to retire through injury while nine-over par for Friday's round.\"Unfortunately he's pulled out this week, which gives me an opportunity to try to claw back some ground that I lost last week in Ireland,\" said McIlroy, who is at eight under overall.\"This weekend is a big weekend for me to pick up a few points and make the race even tighter going into these last few weeks. I'm in a great spot heading into the weekend.\"Gerard made five birdies and an eagle in a flawless round of 65 to leave him two shots clear of Australia's Adam Scott and Italian Filippo Celli, who are at 10 under. US PGA Championship winner Aaron Rai, who played alongside McIlroy and Open champion Ryan Fox in a marquee group, also enjoyed a birdie-birdie finish as he signed for a 69. The Englishman, who had shared the lead overnight, was at nine under alongside Spain's Manuel Elvira, with McIlroy and Fox among a quintet of players a further shot back.Five-time major champion Brooks Koepka was among the big names to miss the cut at one over. Englishman Tommy Fleetwood will also miss the weekend after a 74 left him at three over, while Spain's Jon Rahm endured another day to forget, following up a six-over-par 78 on Thursday with a 77 to end 138th and last of all those to finish on Friday.",
      "source": "BBC",
