      "id": "bbc-2026-09-18-tailgate-parties-marching-bands-and-ted-lasso-wembley-hosts-",
      "title": "Tailgate parties, marching bands... and Ted Lasso? Wembley hosts US college game",
      "summary": "Wembley will host its first American college football game on Saturday and one of Kansas' most famous fans could be among the 60,000 crowd.",
      "body": "Virtually all NFL players come via the US college system, and in many parts of America, college football is more popular than the NFL.College games often have bigger crowds - eight of the world's 10 biggest stadiums are for college football, all holding more than 100,000.The fans are also more passionate as support is often passed down from one generation to another, and multiple generations attend the same school. Kansas player Calvin Clements, for example, is a fifth-generation Jayhawk.Actor Paul Rudd is also a Jayhawk, while lifelong fan Jason Sudeikis grew up in Kansas and frequently weaves KU references into his work.\"They're very spirited, and you get the tribalism, so it's the closest thing we have to European football fanbases,\" Dubiski told BBC Sport.Both the NFL and college football have tailgate parties, where fans gather in the car park  and eat and drink for hours before the game.But college football fans chant during games, which feature marching bands and spirit squads - made up of cheerleaders, dancers and mascots.\"We want the game to showcase what we think is one of the greatest sports in America,\" said Yormark.\"There's something special about college football - the pageantry, the traditions, the fandom. It's dynamic - there's a lot of energy and passion. It truly is an experience.\"According to the NFL, 14 million people in the UK identify as fans of America's biggest sports league.UJC organisers have tried to tap into that fanbase and the UK infrastructure the NFL has built, but also appeal to younger fans.It  formed partnerships with the British American Football Association (Bafa), British Universities and Colleges Sport (BUCS), and the Jacksonville Jaguars, who host an annual NFL game at Wembley.About 60,000 tickets have been sold and 15,000 fans are travelling from the US, with student sections to help create the typical college gameday atmosphere.\"We're really pleased with that in year one,\" said Dubiski. \"We never expected to sell the stadium out, and 60,000 is higher than the average Big 12 attendance.\"Dubiski agreed an exclusive multi-year deal with Wembley and is already working on games in the next three years. He might expand to other European cities and bring basketball games too.Yormark believes a positive experience for fans and players would constitute success, plus an exciting game.Former NFL quarterback Robert Griffin III, now a college football analyst with Fox Sports, agrees that \"we've got to bring excitement\".\"The way to encourage fandom is to create moments, so I'm happy the two teams coming over know how to score points - and score them in bunches,\" he said.Griffin also believes college football could ultimately be more successful overseas than the NFL.\"The fandom around college football is better, it just is,\" he added. \"It's more similar to world football so there's a better opportunity to connect, also because there's more teams. In the NFL there's 32, but in college football there's 400 you can fall in love with.\"And if you want to be a real NFL fan, you have to be a college football fan because that's the feeder system into the league and you get to watch these guys develop.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
