      "id": "theverge-2026-09-16-apple-might-make-servers-again-to-cash-in-on-the-ai-rush",
      "title": "Apple might make servers again to cash in on the AI rush",
      "summary": "According to The Information, Apple is planning to get back into the server game and might just pair up with Nvidia to make it happen. Apple retired its Xserve line in 2011 and has largely left enterprise machines to other manufacturers since. But the growing demand for compute power as the AI industry continues to expand has apparently led the company to believe there is an opening for its powerf",
      "body": "According to The Information, Apple is planning to get back into the server game and might just pair up with Nvidia to make it happen. Apple retired its Xserve line in 2011 and has largely left enterprise machines to other manufacturers since. But the growing demand for compute power as the AI industry continues to expand has apparently led the company to believe there is an opening for its powerful but efficient ARM-based M processors. Its Mac Mini and Mac Studio have proven popular with AI developers, which has led to shortages.\nThe server product, whether it’s called Xserve or something else, likely won’t debut until 2029. Word is that it will run on two or four (depending on the model) M8 Ultra chips. Apple only just announced the M6 in August, so the M8 line is a ways out.\nIt’s also rumored that Apple’s servers could incorporate Nvidia’s NVLink Fusion tech, which connects multiple chips together so they can operate as one. While the two companies have historically had a frosty relationship behind the scenes, recently Apple turned to Nvidia chips to power its revamped Siri servers. Incorporating NVLink could lead to further collaboration between the two, but plans could still change.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Apple",
        "Apple Rumors",
        "News",
        "Nvidia",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Terrence O’Brien",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-16T17:20:29.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/03/STK071_APPLE_I.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/996321/apple-servers-ai-nvidia",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-16-your-health-age-is-fake",
      "title": "Your ‘health age’ is fake",
      "summary": "My villain origin story is my Whoop age. How do you age at 0.9 times your age and end up four to five years older than you are? | Photo by Amelia Holowaty Krales / The Verge\t\n\nThis is Optimizer, a weekly newsletter sent from Verge senior reviewer Victoria Song that dissects and discusses the latest gizmos and potions that swear they're going to change your life. Opt in for Optimizer here.\nDuring l",
      "body": "During last week’s Apple Watch announcement, I let out a distinct “Noooo!” in the Steve Jobs Theater. Along with a new readiness metric and a redesigned Health app, Apple had just announced that later this year, it’d be introducing a new longevity feature called Health Age.&nbsp;\nThis is a familiar concept in the wearable and health tech world. The idea is that if you put effort into your health and fitness, your physiological age could be “younger” than your chronological age. (Or older if you’re not putting in the effort.) Whoop has “Whoop age.” Oura has a “cardiovascular age” metric. My Withings scale just introduced various “longevity intelligence” features that include separate “heart age” and “vascular age” metrics. Garmin also has a “fitness age score.” At CES, I sat in front of a so-called longevity mirror that measured the blood flow in my face from a 30-second selfie to estimate my “physiological age” and my “facial skin age.” Earlier this week, I woke up and found out that my $5,000 smart mattress topper is also estimating my “biological age.”\nIf you ask Whoop, I’m supposedly 42. Over the past two years, Oura says my heart has been anywhere from seven years younger to 1.5 years younger than my chronological age. (It’s currently sitting at two years younger.) Withings says my vascular age is a year older than I am, while my heart is four years younger. Garmin says my fitness age is 36.5 years old. That longevity mirror? My facial skin age was apparently 26 (sunscreen, baby!), while my physiological age was 35. My very rude bed said I’m “trending older” this week at 38.7 years old.&nbsp;\nMy actual age is 38 years old — and that’s what I’m sticking with.\nI’m not trying to rain on Apple’s (or other wearable makers’) parade. Frankly, I won’t rule on Apple’s version of the feature until I can test it for myself. I bring this all up to illustrate a point about when this type of feature can be genuinely helpful — and when you should treat it with a healthy dose of skepticism. As with most metrics, it’s all in the framing.\nIt’s true that through a healthy diet, good sleeping habits, and regular exercise, your physiological condition might be better than that of other people who are the same age and fit within a similar demographic. Depending on how a company defines its various longevity-related age scores, your results can identify areas where you can improve. For example, my Whoop age is somewhat cursed because it heavily weights strength training workouts in the algorithm. The last time I tested Whoop, I wasn’t going super hard on lifting heavy and perhaps was a bit too into cardio. If I wanted my Whoop age to improve, lifting weights would’ve been an easy way to tilt that number in my favor.\n[Image: What do you mean I will be 80.6 years old when I’m 80?! https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/IMG_2608.png?quality=90&strip=all]\nObjectively, recalibrating my training habits to be more balanced would be a good thing for my long-term health. If you leave it exactly at that, there’s nothing wrong with this type of feature. But culturally speaking, that’s not how these “health age” scores work.&nbsp;\nThe way I’ve seen people talk about this metric largely hews toward fluffing up your ego. If your health age is older, you probably don’t talk about it. It’s “shameful” even though, really, it’s not. That’s because culturally — at least in the West — getting old isn’t considered a good thing. If your health age is younger, it becomes something to bandy about. Apple’s vice president of health, Sumbul Desai, even says in this Instagram Reel that Health Age could be something you brag about to your friends. That explains why tech entrepreneur Bryan Johnson, famous for his quest to never die, would post this absurd take on X about how aliens would struggle to distinguish him from his teenage son, alongside a chart titled “Is Bryan Johnson More 18 or 49?” Johnson is 49; he looks a little younger, maybe, and his metrics might be comparable to someone much younger on average, but his current state is ultimately just what 49 looks like for him.&nbsp;\nFurthermore, your wearable-determined age is oftentimes not capturing the whole picture. You could game the Whoop Age metric to the letter, get your annual physical, and find out that your cholesterol level is through the roof. A reader once related this exact story to me. You could optimize all the controllable lifestyle health factors — diet, exercise, and sleep — like Bryan Johnson and still end up with autoimmune gastritis, as he has. Some of your health is encoded in your genetics, and while you can better the odds, your ultimate fate is not always something you can decide. We all know of someone who was healthy as a horse and died tragically young because of a freak accident.&nbsp;\nPersonally, my various health ages have always indicated I’m moderately younger than my chronological age. But even then, that’s not an accurate picture of how I’m aging either. I’m definitely fitter now at 38 than I was at 18. As a teen, I couldn’t even run a mile nonstop, and in my 30s, I have multiple half-marathons under my belt. But my bones also snap, crackle, and pop more. I can’t pull all-nighters without caffeine like I used to, even though I eat much healthier. It doesn’t matter what potions I slather on my hair or what supplements I take; the grays keep coming. I was fitter last year, and my wearables all praised my consistent training to the point that I kept getting injured. I’m less fit this year. I’ve lost roughly 5 pounds of muscle, if smart scales are to be believed, but my metabolic biomarkers are all much, much, much healthier. To the point where earlier this week, my doctor patted me on the back and said she saw all the effort I’d put in over the past year. Yet until recently, my wearables were all convinced my health was in a death spiral.&nbsp;\nHealth ages, I genuinely believe, were meant to be a helpful way of visualizing how small lifestyle changes can improve overall health as you age. But because they’re all defined differently and use a range of parameters, they’ve become these odd “scores” that can pass faulty judgments on how well you’re doing. I look at my hodgepodge of “ages,” and I can’t tell you what any of it truly means.\n[Image: I am proudly 38 years old in this photo and whatever my hair’s doin’, I feel pretty alright. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/07/268639_Meta_Adventurer_glasses_AKrales_0237.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nNot to get too philosophical, but time, as far as we know it, can only move forward. My rude bed is kind of right. Every week, I will be trending older because that’s simply how time works. No matter the wearable metric or magic new drug, none of us is Benjamin Buttoning our way out of that fact.&nbsp;\nWhich brings me to my current philosophy: I am irreversibly 38, and am doing pretty alright if you ask me.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Column",
        "Gadgets",
        "Health",
        "Optimizer",
        "Science",
        "Wearable"
      ],
      "author": "Victoria Song",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-16T17:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/06/257818_Whoop_5_AKrales_0175.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/column/995939/optimizer-health-age-wearables-longevity",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-16-google-will-now-let-any-ai-agent-run-your-smart-home",
      "title": "Google will now let any AI agent run your smart home",
      "summary": "Google is inviting third-party agents, including Claude and Open Claw, into Google Home. | Photo by Jennifer Pattison Tuohy / The Verge\t\n\nGoogle is opening up its smart home to AI agents, letting tools like Claude and Open Claw access and control your connected devices and analyze your home's data using the standardized Model Context Protocol. \nGoogle Home MCP is a new integration that lets third-",
      "body": "Google is opening up its smart home to AI agents, letting tools like Claude and Open Claw access and control your connected devices and analyze your home’s data using the standardized Model Context Protocol.&nbsp;\nGoogle Home MCP is a new integration that lets third-party AI agents control and monitor your smart home and act on your behalf. It “allows any AI agents that support MCP, including Google Antigravity, Claude, Hermes or Open Claw, to securely work with all of the devices and event history in your Google Home ecosystem,” Taylor Lehman, group product manager at Google Home & Nest, said in a blog post.\nAccording to Lehman, Home MCP integration will connect your AI agent of choice to real-world events, enabling a host of new capabilities. From things like cross-camera analysis — so you could ask your agent what your kid did when they got home from school — to using device state history to figure out how many loads of laundry you did last week or how long the lights were left on. It will also allow your agent to interact with you via voice, for example, by sending an audio message over your Google Home speaker when it has finished a task. You can also have your agent create a custom dashboard to control your Google Home.\n[Image: Google Home smart speakers will still be controlled by Gemini for Home, but with MCP enabled third-party AI agents will be able to send voice messages to you through them. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/06/268614_Google_Home_Speaker_JTuohy_0015.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThis doesn’t replace Google’s new Gemini for Home assistant, which remains Google’s interface for interacting with Google Home through the Home app and Google Nest smart speakers, following the demise of Google Assistant. Instead, MCP integration adds another layer of control, allowing third-party AI agents to interact directly with devices connected to Google Home through their own interfaces.&nbsp;\nAt launch, availability is limited to Google Home Premium Advanced users in the US ($20/month or $200/year), with access rolling out in the coming weeks. Setup requires creating a Google Cloud project and configuring it to use the Home MCP.&nbsp;\nGiving an AI agent access to real-world devices like smart locks, thermostats, and core infrastructure like HVAC systems and appliances raises obvious questions about security, privacy, and safety. Google says Home MCP enforces rate limits and safety protections; for example, it won’t allow an agent to unlock doors. However, depending on your agent, “connecting it to Home MCP can result in unexpected or even undesired behavior,” said Lehman, who recommends reviewing Google’s developer policies and terms of service.\nHome Assistant, an open-source smart home platform, has implemented a similar MCP integration that shows what you can do when you give an agent access to your smart home system. I tested this last year to use Claude to vibe-code my smart home, and it easily handled some of the more complex tasks that most interfaces — and users, for that matter — struggle with, including troubleshooting, creating automations, designing a dashboard, and setting up more advanced configurations, all using natural language.&nbsp;\nThe biggest change here isn’t that Google Home can now turn your lights on and off through your Claude app; it’s that Claude — or your agent of choice — has access to the underlying data and control layer of your home. It’s a step toward building a whole home computer for your smart home that can understand what’s happening in it, reason across its history, and take action on your behalf. And if this last week wasn’t dominated by headlines about how AI will kill us all, I’d be really excited.&nbsp;\nStill, there are real advantages to giving an AI agent access to your smart home. It can reason across the data your home generates and act on it in ways traditional smart home systems generally can’t, moving the smart home from command and control toward something more contextual and proactive, closer to actual intelligence. A system that can troubleshoot a device, suggest improvements to automations, and track and identify patterns across your home is much more useful than one that just says “I’m sorry, I can’t do that.” With Home MCP integration, Google is bringing these capabilities into Google Home.\n[Image: Claude created this dashboard for me in Home Assistant using MCP. With Google Home MCP enabled, it should be able to do something similar for my Google Home. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Screenshot-2026-09-16-at-12.40.50-PM.png?quality=90&strip=all]\nWhile consumer-facing tools like Claude will be useful for smart home tinkerers, the bigger picture here is Google’s developer play. Over the last couple of years, Google has increasingly positioned itself as the smart home’s infrastructure layer. It launched API access to its smart home in 2024 and more recently made Gemini for Home a full-stack AI offering. All of this points toward a more B2B2C strategy: Google can provide the AI and smart home infrastructure while other companies build consumer-facing services and products on top.&nbsp;\nWith this week’s announcement, Google has also opened a path for developers to use its agentic Antigravity coding tool to build custom AI agents for the smart home that tap into Google Home device data and control layer. But even if developers use a different agent, as long as they’re using Google Home, it’s a win for Google. This way, Google becomes the smart home infrastructure layer, in much the same way AWS became infrastructure for internet and software businesses.&nbsp;\nWhether smart home developers will buy into Google’s promises here is a big question. Based on the number of smart home platforms the company has launched and let die over the years — Android @ Home, Weave, Project Brillo, Works with Nest, Google Assistant — developers will have to decide whether Google has finally settled on a smart home platform it intends to stick with.",
